#{[17592186420911 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420911 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420911 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420911 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/01/01d8d85064253b9241b1fd93c22381897f23556d6e02c5c2c0161c94cee3bbdf.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420911 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1cc7cc51-6281-53f8-b74f-55c2a0b2fa6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420911 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *in* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb4fd4068760bb9132052b26508f0e1e0c02c45d425770de804b17ce7c512919.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/46/46afd4cde01c95388c9daefbef0668ef696d5817ab9ea3614982edf58a9bdf55.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420912 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420912 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420912 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420912 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/af/afaec113101f1a63134d2b1cb5c9744b93f9b84dabafaa205780d2dc5b49a7bc.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420912 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e18756f-1e50-5f09-be71-26f001c4f68e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420912 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/6b/6b24ece279cc480f20d7a4dbc65f7a5257af71f22688b1416c73e3b40673a321.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/9e/9e8527d26f0f2bd5372d8643f8f9321ec022bb0079e1fc5b23be4978f436613c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420913 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420913 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420913 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420913 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1b/1b08e4cc9dd6ebde7f4bf544d7a76c4706dbd69a2c31d4c0fda8f816d4f871b5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+(x-y)^{2}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C$  shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420913 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71fbebcb-93ff-5be9-84c7-d1676b609367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420913 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{2}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=j.$ \n\n![](images/5d/5d0e9699f75e1f3f727c5c771fdce1a66311496cb55d31c0aac789901feed0cb.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}((x+y)^{2}\\,i+(x-y)^{2}\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x-y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(x-y)^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420914 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420914 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420914 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420914 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0f/0f1d25942ae680c068b587f38bfbc968c8850b8fa2db2003e994e1ec32dad892.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420914 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "765f030b-c4cb-527c-8c26-c947b0ace6a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420914 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7acc3028de66ae71ae4e425da639e51a357a77f2b0a345290592cac91ba17d3.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/04/04639a0074aa4607fa57bfe589aad1780f8da6f573d8589a68c2fdb569b5a129.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420915 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420915 #edb/ref 1069 "q-226132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420915 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420915 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f4/f413fd04759cdb02118b320fea2f678712681a57f5b08e1bcb57a6843c0c80f0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=4x\\,i+4y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420915 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f447fce-ea84-5549-9b80-d5d43d163662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420915 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/87/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(4x\\,i+4y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =4{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12(3-(-3)) \\\\  & =12\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 72=288.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420916 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420916 #edb/ref 1069 "q-226136" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420916 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420916 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9d/9da6bdb9ab5f93d0b0d11dfd60ab2b647380a94ce337c2314ec3504cabafa889.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=4x\\,i-3y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420916 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab737fa4-bfd8-53e2-9695-4ad4cbdcb6c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420916 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(4x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(4x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-6.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-6)=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420917 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420917 #edb/ref 1069 "q-226115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420917 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420917 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/57/575463a6fbe18d3f9b805e1a9c3d8e52bedd84a8db3a3c90d87381b8cb7abf74.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-4x\\,i-4y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420917 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4ef9955-c544-5a0b-9748-af661e7750b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420917 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/eb/ebe05a522f9f46acfed63e29927b46f41d78483b5df5e7a15a15ae5f33abd2f4.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-4x\\,i-4y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-8)=-32.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
